## Why the current design is failing

The deepest design problem is that your repo currently appears to treat Copilot instructions as if they were equivalent to policy enforcement. GitHub’s docs say repository instructions are added to requests and can be verified in references, but that same doc set also says multiple instruction layers can apply at once and warns users to avoid conflicting sets of instructions. In practice, that means long global instructions plus large agent prompts plus many playbooks is not a stronger control system; it is often just a noisier one. Your repo-wide file appears to combine immutable rules, process routing, hook-runtime descriptions, KOSH behavior, and agent workflow requirements into a single large always-on surface. That is likely part of why the experience feels bloated. Repository evidence: `.github/copilot-instructions.md:1-145`. citeturn19view5turn30view1turn28view3

A second cause is that your current persona model is textual rather than runtime-native. GitHub custom agents are documented as behavioral prompt profiles, not as built-in finite-state machines with guaranteed user-facing transition announcements. So if you want persona transitions to happen *reliably*, you need to add a validator that checks the response contract and blocks finalization when persona labels are missing. GitHub’s hook reference gives you the exact primitive for that: `agentStop` and `subagentStop` can return `{"decision":"block","reason":"..."}` to force another turn. Without that, the persona rule is aspirational. Repository evidence: `.github/agents/codemindIDEagent.agent.md:30-69`. citeturn47view2turn27view2turn27view7

A third cause is the Sonar execution gap. The repo says SonarQube for IDE / SonarLint should run when configured, and there is a visible `sonar_adapter.py`. But there is no visible proof in the screenshots that edit or create operations are bound to a Sonar invocation, nor that stop events are blocked when Sonar policy is violated. GitHub hooks can absolutely support this pattern: `postToolUse` can append additional context or stage analysis state, and `agentStop` can block finalization. SonarQube’s GitHub Actions guidance then gives you the hard CI gate. This combination matches your needs much better than treating Sonar as a best-effort instruction inside the Quality Enforcer text. Repository evidence: `.github/hooks/sonar_adapter.py` visible; `.github/agents/codemindIDEagent.agent.md:47-52`. citeturn27view5turn41view4turn45view0turn7view1

A fourth cause is performance pressure from both instructions and hooks. GitHub states plainly that hooks run synchronously and block agent execution, and recommends tight execution budgets, caching, and backgrounding expensive work. Your current visible design asks the agent to do a lot before implementation begins and appears to include many governance files, playbooks, and graph artifacts in the same repository. That means the runtime should be explicitly designed to do the **minimum** necessary on prompt submission and tool use, then defer heavier checks to stop events and CI. Otherwise your own governance layer becomes the latency source. Repository evidence: `.github/agents/codemindIDEagent.agent.md:18-28`; `.github/copilot-instructions.md:34-145`; root explorer screenshot. citeturn25view0turn25view4turn34view0

The token question also matters analytically because it is easy to solve poorly. GitHub’s billing and usage docs talk about prompt requests, premium requests, model multipliers, weekly token limits, plan mode reducing overall token consumption, and parallel workflows increasing token usage. But the documented hook payloads for prompt submission, tool use, and stop events contain prompt text, tool arguments, tool results, timestamps, and transcript paths—not explicit token counters. So if you implement “token consumed by Copilot in each iteration,” the robust design is a truthful **metering service** that reports `exact`, `estimated`, or `unavailable`, rather than a fake exact number. citeturn33view1turn33view2turn34view0turn35view2turn35view3

