## Recommended target design

The cleanest redesign is to preserve your current architecture but split it into four control layers. Keep `.github/copilot-instructions.md` as the small global constitution. Keep `codemindIDEagent.agent.md` as the focused behavioral profile and response contract. Move directory-specific rules into `.github/instructions/**/*.instructions.md` so they only apply when relevant. Then put actual policy execution in `.github/hooks/*.json` calling the Python modules you already have. GitHub explicitly supports repo-wide instructions, path-specific instruction files, prompt files for reusable tasks, and custom agent profiles with optional `tools`, `model`, and `target` properties. citeturn19view5turn30view4turn28view3turn47view2turn47view4

A practical persona-enforcement strategy is to make the required output contract machine-checkable. For significant tasks, require the response to begin with a single line like `Active persona: Developer — implementing approved governance changes`, followed by `selected_playbooks`, `success_criteria`, and, at task end, `sonar_status`, `token_usage`, `audit_verdict`, and `kosh_ref`. Then add an `agentStop` hook that parses the transcript for those fields and returns a blocking reason when they are absent. GitHub’s documented `agentStop` blocking mechanism is exactly designed for this kind of “revise before completion” policy. citeturn27view2turn27view7turn35view3

A practical routing-and-bloat strategy is to stop making the agent rediscover the full playbook universe on every turn. Instead, create or generate a compact playbook manifest that maps request classes to minimal chains, and make `resolver.py` cache it. At the same time, give the resolver and any repo-scan logic an ignore list for `my_venv/`, `.pytest_cache/`, `__pycache__/`, `generated/`, `graph-out/`, and `.codegraph-runtime/`, plus anything else bulky and low-value. GitHub’s prompt-files and path-specific instruction support mean you can move long task-specific material out of the global prompt and only load it when the request or edited path requires it. GitHub also notes that custom agent prompts can be large, but large is not the same as efficient. citeturn30view4turn28view2turn28view3turn47view2turn25view0

For Sonar, the strongest operational design is a two-stage gate. Locally, after `edit` or `create`, stage changed files and run a fast, bounded Sonar adapter pass when the runtime is available, returning a structured status such as `passed`, `failed`, or `unavailable_local`. At finalization, the stop hook should block if local Sonar found threshold-breaching issues. In CI, run the full SonarQube scan and make the resulting governance or quality check a required branch check. This preserves your desire for standards enforcement without making every small local event unbearably slow. Repository evidence: `.github/hooks/sonar_adapter.py` visible; `.github/agents/codemindIDEagent.agent.md:47-52`. citeturn45view0turn7view1turn25view0

For token metering, the right design is a sidecar rather than a fantasy. Use `userPromptSubmitted` to meter prompt size, `postToolUse` to meter tool-output size and stage changed files, and `agentStop` to parse the transcript response text. If the runtime ever exposes actual usage metadata, record `metering_mode: exact`; otherwise use a tokenizer-backed estimate with a conservative fallback and record `metering_mode: estimated`. Because GitHub’s docs explicitly discuss request counts and token limits but not per-hook token fields, a truthful estimate is the governance-safe answer. citeturn33view2turn34view0turn35view2turn35view3

